WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
How can two pure-breeding white-eyed flies have red-eyed offspring?

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.160
Watch their chromosome copies enter one shared cell.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.420
Each parent carries a different hidden loss.

4
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.140
The offspring eye begins white, waiting to see whether normal function returns.

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.293
A red eye needs several genes working together.

6
00:00:22.593 --> 00:00:24.013
Some genes make pigments.

7
00:00:25.293 --> 00:00:27.253
Others move pigments into granules.

8
00:00:28.413 --> 00:00:31.193
A missing shared transporter leaves the eye white.

9
00:00:32.493 --> 00:00:34.453
So does blocking both pigment pathways.

10
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.033
Appearance alone cannot tell which gene broke.

11
00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:41.403
An allele is one version of a gene.

12
00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:47.343
A diploid cell carries paired chromosome copies, so it can carry paired alleles.

13
00:00:48.543 --> 00:00:52.563
For a recessive loss, one working allele usually makes enough product.

14
00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:56.583
That product works at its normal place in the cell.

15
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:00.617
First check each mutation against wild type.

16
00:01:01.777 --> 00:01:06.997
If a working copy restores the ordinary phenotype, the mutation is recessive.

17
00:01:08.077 --> 00:01:11.357
That check matters because complementation reads rescue.

18
00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:16.497
A dominant mutation can keep the cell mutant beside any partner.

19
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:23.317
Now combine the checked recessive mutations on opposite chromosome copies in one

20
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:26.537
diploid cell. This arrangement is trans.

21
00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:34.217
One chromosome has broken gene A and working gene B. The other has working A and

22
00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:35.217
broken B.

23
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:38.707
Will this cell recover its red eye, or stay white?

24
00:01:39.947 --> 00:01:42.567
Follow the working copies, not the broken ones.

25
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:47.167
Decide whether the shared cell now has every function it needs.

26
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.823
The working A copy supplies the blue function.

27
00:01:57.123 --> 00:01:59.723
The working B copy supplies the gold function.

28
00:02:00.943 --> 00:02:04.343
With both functions present in the cell, red pigment appears.

29
00:02:05.923 --> 00:02:08.863
Normal function returns, so the mutations complement.

30
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:16.653
Reset the cell. Now both chromosomes carry broken alleles of gene A, while gene B

31
00:02:16.653 --> 00:02:19.713
works. No working A product appears.

32
00:02:21.093 --> 00:02:26.053
The eye stays white, so these mutations fail to complement under the simple model.

33
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:31.513
Mutations that fail with one another form a complementation group.

34
00:02:32.693 --> 00:02:35.773
In the simple model, a group usually marks one gene.

35
00:02:37.193 --> 00:02:41.993
Testing many pairs turns one shared phenotype into groups, revealing how many

36
00:02:41.993 --> 00:02:43.853
functions the screen happened to hit.

37
00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:46.397
The rule has boundaries.

38
00:02:47.897 --> 00:02:51.997
Intragenic complementation can rescue different alleles of one gene.

39
00:02:53.477 --> 00:02:56.717
Mutations in different genes can sometimes fail together.

40
00:02:57.957 --> 00:02:59.837
Those exceptions refine the test.

41
00:03:01.037 --> 00:03:04.757
They do not change what a clean recessive result usually means.

42
00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:07.867
Always name what the test measured.

43
00:03:09.127 --> 00:03:13.527
A red eye is observed rescue, not direct proof of chromosome position.

44
00:03:15.007 --> 00:03:16.667
Mapping asks where mutations sit.

45
00:03:18.147 --> 00:03:22.507
Complementation asks whether their functions can supply each other in one cell.
